{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Phenomenological Thinking.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Phenomenological Thinking.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"phenomenological thinking phenomenological thinking","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Phenomenological Thinking.md","fileName":"Phenomenological Thinking.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Phenomenological Thinking","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 14:36\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Systems Foundations]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Systems Foundations","System","Systems Thinking","advancedgamedesign.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Phenomenological Thinking.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Phenomenological Thinking.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"phenomenological thinking phenomenological thinking","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Phenomenological Thinking.md","fileName":"Phenomenological Thinking.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Phenomenological Thinking","heading":"Phenomenological Thinking","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Phenomenological thinking treats observed events as separate phenomena without necessarily looking for a deeper structure connecting them. It is useful for noticing what occurs, but it can miss the interactions and loops that produce recurring behavior.\n\nWhen an apparent event is an expression of a [[System]], a purely phenomenological view may address symptoms rather than causes. [[Systems Thinking]] extends observation by asking how parts and feedback generate the phenomenon.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Systems Foundations","System","Systems Thinking","advancedgamedesign.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Phenomenological Thinking.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Phenomenological Thinking.